                         HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   Attendants step on the beat. Hands land on the beat. A person
grabbing for you is a person doing a thing on a count.
   I wasn’t going to be on the count.
   I stepped on the off-beat, a hair late, and the attendant’s second
hand closed on nothing, and his weight went with it. He stumbled for‐
ward with an expression of mild betrayal. “Sorry,” I said, and meant it.
“Perdón.”
   “Left,” said Manny, behind me.
   He’d stood up. He had the conga against his chest with both hands,
tuxedo split at the shoulder, bow tie askew, and he did the one thing in
the world he is better at than anyone alive. He played the other num‐
ber.
   Not seven this time. Three against two, a rolling, tumbling, crooked
pattern, three beats laid over two like a hand over a hand, the oldest
conversation in Afro-Caribbean music. He slapped it out on the drum‐
head with his palms and the heels of his hands, loud, in the middle of
the Armory, while eleven hundred people breathed rain.
   The attendants’ feet faltered.
   I watched it travel down the wall like a ripple. Every gray polo with
a click in his ear was being told left, and every ear on the outside of his
head was being told something else, and for half a second the line of
staff had two instructions and no way to choose. They bumped. One
stepped on another’s heel. A woman in a gray polo said “Excuse me,” to
a man in a gray polo with great sincerity. Somebody’s earbud popped
out and rolled across the carpet.
   It was the funniest thing I’d ever seen. It looked like a drill team that
had met a drum line in an alley.
   “Don’t laugh,” said Manny, drumming. “It’s violent.”



We went through them.
   I got through on perdón. I said it eleven times in the first twenty
yards. Perdón, perdón, con permiso. We threaded the aisle with a stone on
a trolley and a drum and a man in a tuxedo, past ushers and donors




